          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em
              symplectic
              = succeed "-groupoid" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} smooth ∞-groupoid
            equipped with a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-plectic+form"} n-plectic form
          %p
            This is the generalization of the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+symplectic+geometry"} higher symplectic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of the notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+Lie+n-algebroid"} symplectic L-∞ algebroid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal
            way.
          %p
            Notice that every symplectic manifold is in particular a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            and that the structure of a Poisson manifold is equivalently encoded in the corresponding
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroid"} Poisson Lie algebroid
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of such a Poisson Lie algebroid. Therefore, strictly speaking, already “ordinary” symplectic geometry secretly involves
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoid
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization+of+symplectic+groupoids"} geometric quantization of symplectic groupoids
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            For any
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+Lie+n-algebroid"} symplectic Lie n-algebroid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔓
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathfrak{P}, \omega)
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E+algebroid"} L-∞ algebroid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔓
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{P}
            that is equipped with a quadratic and non-degenerate
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
          %p
            Under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔓
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{P}
            integrates to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoid"} smooth n-groupoid
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mi n
                    %mi exp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔓
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau_n \exp(\mathfrak{P})
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Weil+homomorphism"} ∞-Chern-Weil homomorphism
            the invariant polynomial induces an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoid+--+structures"} differential form on an ∞-groupoid
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi τ
                    %mi n
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔓
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mo ♭
                    %mi dR
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\omega : \tau_n \exp(\mathfrak{P}) \to \flat_{dR} \mathbf{B}^{n+2} \mathbb{R}
          %p
            representing a class
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ∈
                    %msubsup
                      %mi H
                      %mi dR
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mi n
                    %mi exp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔓
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\omega] \in H^{n+2}_{dR}(\tau_n \exp(\mathfrak{P}))
          %p Let
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SymplSmooth
                  %mn ∞
                  %mi Grpd
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi Smooth
                  %mn ∞
                  %mi Grpd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mo ♭
                    %mi dR
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  SymplSmooth\infty Grpd
                  \\hookrightarrow
                  Smooth\infty Grpd/(\coprod_{n}\mathbf{\flat}_{dR}\mathbf{B}^{n+2}\mathbb{R})
          %p
            be the full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sub-%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} sub-(∞,1)-category
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/over-%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} over-(∞,1)-topos
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Smooth%E2%88%9EGrpd"} Smooth∞Grpd
            over the de Rham coefficient objects on those objects in the image of this construction.
          %p
            We say an object on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SymplSmooth
                  %mn ∞
                  %mi Grpd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SymplSmooth \infty Grpd
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                symplectic smooth
                = succeed "-groupoid" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            (There are evident variations of this for the ambient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Smooth%E2%88%9EGrpd"} Smooth∞Grpd
            replaced by some variant, such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SynthDiff%E2%88%9EGrpd"} SynthDiff∞Grpd
            or
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SmoothSuper%E2%88%9EGrpd"} SmoothSuper∞Grpd
          %h2#ByLieIntegration Examples
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+Lie+n-algebroid"} symplectic Lie n-algebroid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
            is Lie theoretically an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-Chern-Weil+theory"} infinity-Chern-Weil theory
            it induces a moprhism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi τ
                    %mi n
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mo ♭
                    %mi dR
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\exp(\omega)
                  \:
                  \\tau_n\exp(\mathfrak{a})
                  \\to
                  \\mathbf{\flat}_{dR} \mathbf{B}^{n+2} \mathbb{R}
          %p
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
            to the de Rham coefficient object: this is an
            = succeed "-form" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+2)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} smooth ∞-groupoid
            (as discussed at
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoid+--+structures#StrucDeRham"} smooth ∞-groupoid – structures – de Rham cohomology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(\mathfrak{a})
            with the structure of a symplectic
            = succeed "-groupoid." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p We spell this out in some special cases.
          %h3#__symplectic_manifolds
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 0
            – Symplectic manifolds
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+Lie+n-algebroid"} symplectic Lie 0-algebroid
            is simply a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
          %h3#SymplecticGroupoidsFromPoissonLieAlgebroids
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 1
            – Symplectic groupoids from Poisson Lie algebroids
          %p
            We discuss the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroids"} Poisson Lie algebroids
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoids"} symplectic groupoids
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+geometric+quantization+of+2d+Chern-Simons+theory"} extended geometric quantization of 2d Chern-Simons theory
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔓
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{P}
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroid"} Poisson Lie algebroid
            corresponding to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            that comes from a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
            associated to this is (by the discussion there) supposed to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(X)
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            equipped on its space of morphisms with the differential form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi p
                      %mn 1
                      %mo *
                    %mi ω
                    %mo −
                    %msubsup
                      %mi p
                      %mn 2
                      %mo *
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_1^* \omega - p_2^* \omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_1,p_2
            are the two endpoint projections from paths in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            We demonstrate in the following how this is indeed the result of applying the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Weil+homomorphism"} ∞-Chern-Weil homomorphism
            to this situation.
          %p
            For simplicity we shall start with the simple situation where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            has a global
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Darboux+coordinate+chart"} Darboux coordinate chart
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x^i\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\omega_{i j}\}
            for the components of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
            in these coordinates, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi ω
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\omega^{i j}\}
            for the components of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} inverse
          %p
            Then the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔓
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(\mathfrak{P})
            is generated from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x^i\}
            in degree 0 and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\partial_i\}
            in degree 1, with differential given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi CE
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mi ω
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d_{CE} x^i = - \omega^{i j} \partial_j
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi CE
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi π
                        %mrow
                          %mi j
                          %mi k
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi j
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d_{CE} \partial_i = \frac{\partial \pi^{j k}}{\partial x^i} \partial_j \wedge \partial_k = 0
                  \\,.
          %p
            The differential in the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weil+algebra"} Weil algebra
            is hence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi W
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mi ω
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi j
                  %mo +
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d_{W} x^i = - \omega^{i j} \partial_j + \mathbf{d}x^i
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi W
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d_{W} \partial_i = \mathbf{d} \partial_i
                  \\,.
          %p
            By the discussion at
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroid"} Poisson Lie algebroid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi ω
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∧
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔓
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{\omega} = \mathbf{d} x^i \wedge \mathbf{d} \partial_i \in W(\mathfrak{P})
                  \\,.
          %p Clearly it is useful to introduce a new basis of generators with
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mi ω
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi j
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\partial^i := -\omega^{i j} \partial_j
                  \\,.
          %p In this new basis we have a manifest isomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔓
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔗
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  CE(\mathfrak{P}) = CE(\mathfrak{T}X)
          %p
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+Lie+algebroid"} tangent Lie algebroid
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Therefore the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔓
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{P}
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible"} contractible
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair+groupoid"} pair groupoid
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi τ
                    %mn 1
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔓
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %munder
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mn 2
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\tau_1 \exp(\mathfrak{P}) = \Pi_1(X) = (X \times X \stackrel{\overset{p_2}{\to}}{\underset{p_1}{\to}} X)
                  \\,.
          %p
            It remains to show that the symplectic form on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔓
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{P}
            makes this a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
          %p Notice that in the new basis the invariant polynomial reads
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi ω
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %msub
                            %mi ω
                            %mrow
                              %mi i
                              %mi j
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mi i
                          %mo ∧
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mo ∂
                            %mi j
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi d
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi ω
                            %mrow
                              %mi i
                              %mi j
                          %msup
                            %mo ∂
                            %mi i
                          %mo ∧
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mi j
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  \\mathbf{\omega}
                  \&amp;=
                  \- \omega_{i j} \mathbf{d}x^i \wedge \mathbf{d} \partial^j
                  \\\
                  \&amp; = \mathbf{d}( \omega_{i j} \partial^i \wedge \mathbf{d}x^j)
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p
            and that we may regard this as a morphism of
            = succeed "-algebroids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi ω
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝔗
                  %mi 𝔓
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝔗
                  %msup
                    %mi b
                    %mn 3
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{\omega}
                  \:
                  \\mathfrak{T}\mathfrak{P} \to \mathfrak{T}b^3 \mathbb{R}
          %p
            The corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-Chern-Weil+theory"} infinity-Chern-Weil homomorphism
            that we need to compute is given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-anafunctor"} ∞-anafunctor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi exp
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi 𝔓
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi diff
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi exp
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi ω
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mi exp
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi b
                          %mi ℝ
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi dR
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mo ∫
                                %mrow
                                  %msup
                                    %mi Δ
                                    %mo •
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mo ♭
                            %mi dR
                          %msup
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mn 3
                          %mi ℝ
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mo ≃
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi exp
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi 𝔓
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\exp(\mathfrak{P})_{diff}
                  \&amp;\stackrel{\exp(\mathbf{\omega})}{\to}&amp;
                  \\exp(b \mathbb{R})_{dR}
                  \&amp;\stackrel{\int_{\Delta^\bullet}}{\to}&amp;
                  \\mathbf{\flat}_{dR}\mathbf{B}^3 \mathbb{R}
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\simeq}}
                  \\\
                  \\exp(\mathfrak{P})
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            Over a test space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            in degree 1 an element in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔓
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi diff
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(\mathfrak{P})_{diff}
            is a pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi η
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X^i, \eta^i)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X^i \in C^\infty(U \times \Delta^1)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi η
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi vert
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\eta^i \in \Omega^1_{vert}(U \times \Delta^1)
          %p
            subject to the verticality constraint, which says that along
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^1
            we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                  %mo +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi η
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mn 1
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d_{\Delta^1} X^i + \eta^i_{\Delta^1} = 0
                  \\,.
          %p
            The vertical morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔓
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi diff
                  %mo →
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔓
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(\mathfrak{P})_{diff} \to \exp(\mathfrak{P})
            has in fact a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            whose image is given by those pairs for which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi η
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta^i
            has no leg along
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔓
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(\mathfrak{P})
            by evaluating the top morphism on pairs of this form.
          %p Such a pair is taken by the top morphism to
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mi X
                            %mi i
                          %mo ,
                          %msup
                            %mi η
                            %mi j
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↦
                          %msub
                            %mo ∫
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mi Δ
                                %mn 1
                          %msub
                            %mi ω
                            %mrow
                              %mi i
                              %mi j
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mi X
                                %mi i
                          %mo ∧
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mo ∂
                                %mi j
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %msub
                            %mo ∫
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mi Δ
                                %mn 1
                          %msub
                            %mi ω
                            %mrow
                              %mi i
                              %mi j
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi d
                            %mi dR
                          %msup
                            %mi X
                            %mi i
                          %mo +
                          %msup
                            %mi η
                            %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∧
                          %msub
                            %mi d
                            %mi dR
                          %msup
                            %mi η
                            %mi j
                          %mo ∈
                          %msup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mn 3
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  (X^i, \eta^j)
                  \&amp; \mapsto
                  \\int_{\Delta^1}
                  \\omega_{i j} F_{X^i} \wedge F_{\partial^j}
                  \\\
                  \&amp; =
                  \\int_{\Delta^1}
                  \\omega_{i j} (d_{dR} X^i + \eta^i) \wedge d_{dR} \eta^j
                  \\in
                  \\Omega^3(U)
                  \\end{aligned}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Using the above verticality constraint and the condition that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi η
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta^i
            has no leg along
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi U
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi U
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi j
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\cdots =
                  \\int_{\Delta^1}
                  \\omega_{i j}
                  d_U X^i
                  \\wedge
                  d_U d_{\Delta^1} X^j
                  \\,.
          %p
            By the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stokes+theorem"} Stokes theorem
            the integration over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^1
            yields
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi dR
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∧
                  %msup
                    %mi η
                    %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mn 0
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi dR
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∧
                  %msup
                    %mi η
                    %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\cdots
                  \=
                  \\omega_{i j} d_{dR} x^i \wedge \eta^j|_{0}
                  \-
                  \\omega_{i j} d_{dR} x^i \wedge \eta^j|_{1}
                  \\,.
          %p This completes the proof.
          %h3#__symplectic_2groupoids_from_courant_lie_2algebroids
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 2
            – Symplectic 2-groupoids from Courant Lie 2-algebroids
          %h2#HigherGeometricQuantization
            Geometric quantization of symplectic
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %h3#GeometricQuantizationIdea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            formalizes in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            the concept of a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanical+system"} classical mechanical system
            \. The notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            of a symplectic manifold is one formalization of the general concept in physics of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of such a system to a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanical+system"} quantum mechanical system
            \.
          %p
            Or rather, the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            does not quite capture the most general systems of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifolds"} Poisson manifolds
            \. The original methods of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            become meaningless on a Poisson manifold that is not symplectic.
          %p
            However, a Poisson structure on a manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is equivalent to the structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroid"} Poisson Lie algebroid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔓
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{P}
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %em is
            again symplectic, as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebroid"} Lie algebroid
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+Lie+n-algebroid"} symplectic Lie algebroid
            \.
          %p
            Based on related observations it was suggested that the notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
            (see the references there) should naturally replace that of
            %em symplectic manifold
            for the purposes of geometric quantization to yield a notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization+of+symplectic+groupoids"} geometric quantization of symplectic groupoids
            \.
          %p
            Since a symplectic manifold can be regarded as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+Lie+n-algebroid"} symplectic Lie 0-algebroid
            and also as a symplectic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoid"} smooth 0-groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
          %p
            More or less implicitly, there has been strong evidence that this shift in perspective is substantial: the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            (see there for references) of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            turns out to be constructible in terms of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlator"} correlator
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+sigma-model"} Poisson sigma-model
            associated with the corresponding
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroid"} Poisson Lie algebroid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanical+system"} quantum mechanical system
            that it thus encodes, is a direct reflection of this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            shift of degree – see
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic principle
            for more on this.
          %p
            On general abstract grounds this already suggests that it makes sense to pass via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher categorification
            further to
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Courant+Lie+2-algebroid"} symplectic Lie 2-algebroid
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+Lie+n-algebroid"} symplectic Lie n-algebroid
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-groupoid"} 2-groupoid
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-groupoids"} 3-groupoids
            = succeed "-groupoids." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            Formal hints for such a generalization had been noted in (
            %a{:href => "#Severa"}> Ševera
            ), in particular in its concluding table. More indirect – but all the more noteworthy – hints came from
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multisymplectic+geometry"} multisymplectic geometry
            of degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            more naturally captures the description of
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
            (notice that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            may be understood as
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0+1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+Lie+n-algebroid"} symplectic Lie n-algebroid
            is, while always “binary”, nevertheless a representative of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham cohomology
            in degree
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+2)
          %p
            There is a natural formalization of these higher symplectic structures in the context of any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive (∞,1)-topos
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#FRS"} FRS
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E+algebroid"} L-∞ algebroid
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+theory"} ∞-Lie theory
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Weil+homomorphism"} ∞-Chern-Weil homomorphism
            applies to them.
          %p
            We shall show below that all notions of geometric quantization of symplectic
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"} circle n-bundle with connection
            that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Weil+homomorphism"} ∞-Chern-Weil homomorphism
            assigns to the symplectic form, regarded as an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            – the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AKSZ+sigma-model"} AKSZ sigma-model
            – is that given by the induced
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Simons+theory"} ∞-Chern-Simons functional
          %h3#PrequantumBundles
            Prequantum circle
            = succeed "-bundle" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
          %h4#idea_3 Idea
          %p
            What is called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} (geometric) prequantization
            is a refinement of symplectic 2-forms to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            2-forms on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            %em prequantum line bundle
            for the given symplectic form.
          %p
            This has an evident generalization to closed forms of degree
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+2)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            = succeed "-form" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+2)
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"} circle n-bundle with connection
            \. Since in the context of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} smooth ∞-groupoid
            = succeed "-bundles" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-groupoids," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %strong
              prequantum circle
              = succeed "-bundles" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            on a symplectic
            = succeed "-groupoid." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Moreover, since, as discussed above, the symplectic form on a symplectic
            = succeed "-groupoid" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            under the unrefined
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Weil+homomorphism"} ∞-Chern-Weil homomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
